Q: How to toggle icon/image of Bootstrap button with jQuery and data attributes? A: <p>Sorted now. Solution was to use jQuery's html() method to get/set the inner HTML, rather than the text() method:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;button
    class=&quot;btn btn-sm btn-primary&quot;
    title=&quot;Click to read more&quot;
    data-bs-toggle=&quot;collapse&quot;
    data-bs-target=&quot;#collapse1&quot;
    type=&quot;button&quot;
    aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot;
    aria-controls=&quot;collapse1&quot;
    data-text-alt=&quot;Show less &lt;i class='fa fa-caret-up'&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&quot;
    &gt;Show more &lt;i class=&quot;fa fa-caret-down&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/button&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// Save current button text (inner HTML) to 'text-original'
$(this).data('text-original', $(this).html())

// Set button inner HTML to 'text-alt'
.html($(this).data('text-alt'))

// Set 'text-alt' to 'text-original'
.data('text-alt', $(this).data('text-original'));
</code></pre>

Q: Controlling the placement of &lt;nav&gt; element A: <p>It was a combination of multiple things, and many of the answers here helped solve the problem. I suggest reading them all if you experience a similar problem. First of all adding the following to the CSS helped remove any unnecessary offsets in the <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> field (thanks Markai):</p>

<pre><code>nav#nav01{
    padding: 0;
}
</code></pre>

<p>The remainder of the issue related to the padding in <code>ul#menu</code> in the CSS. I added a 9 pixels <em>vertical</em> padding and a 0 pixel <em>horizontal</em> padding. Combined with the 1 pixel border I also added, this gives a combined vertical offset of 10 pixels, matching the vertical padding in <code>ul#menu li a</code> placing the tabs perfectly where they should be. Here is the changed parts of the CSS:</p>

<pre><code>nav#nav001 {
    padding: 0;
}

ul#menu {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    display: block;
    padding: 9px 0px;
}

ul#menu li a {
    border: 1px solid;
    margin: 0px;
    padding: 10px 20px;
    background-color: #ffffff;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: #696969;
    border-radius: 4px 4px 0 0;
}
</code></pre>

<p>Or you can see the full code and effect in the much hated, much loved <a href="http://jsfiddle.net/Lca6ayna/2/" rel="nofollow">JSFiddle</a> that I have created. Thank you very much for you help everyone!</p>
